  "estimatedMinutes": null,
  "statement": "Consider the parabola consisting of the points \\((x, y)\\) in the real plane satisfying\n\n\\[(y+x)=(y-x)^{2}+3(y-x)+3 .\\]\n\nFind the minimum possible value of \\(y\\).",
  "answerChoices": [],
  "answer": {
    "letter": null,
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.hmmt-nov-theme-2014-p8.hmmt",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "\\(\\quad-\\frac{1}{2}\\) Let \\(w=y-x\\). Adding \\(w\\) to both sides and dividing by two gives\n\n\\[y=\\frac{w^{2}+4 w+3}{2}=\\frac{(w+2)^{2}-1}{2}\\]\n\nwhich is minimized when \\(w=-2\\). This yields \\(y=-\\frac{1}{2}\\).",
